"""
## Purpose and audience

Executable source-to-rich-AST coverage for GPU `shared` declarations,
written for the frontend engineers who own the parser and the flat-AST
bridge, and for GPU-lane reviewers who need proof that `shared let/val/var`
survive parsing with their mutability, initializer and span intact.

## Operator workflow

Run this spec and confirm the Results line reports exactly 1 failed (the
ordinary-identifier canary, see Compatibility). The first four scenarios
are fail-closed tripwires over the real parser: each proves the drafted
`shared` declaration grammar is still rejected, and each carries its
drafted assertions verbatim for the day the grammar lands. The last
scenario parses a fixture where `shared` is used as an ordinary identifier
and pins the decoded statement tags.

## Compatibility and limitations

The drafted surface only partially exists: the frontend parser accepts
`shared val x: T = expr` and `shared var x: T` today, but the drafted
optional-initializer contract is rejected (`shared val x: T` and
`shared let x: T` without an initializer fail with "expected ="),
STMT_SHARED_VAR_DECL / STMT_SHARED_VAL_DECL flat tags are defined nowhere
(grep-verified at authoring time), and no parser path produces the
StmtKind.SharedVal / StmtKind.SharedVar bridge variants, so scenarios one
to four cannot drive the drafted decode path and instead fail closed while
the drafted contract is absent. Scenario five is a legitimate RED:
`var shared = 1` parses as STMT_VAR_DECL, but the following
`shared = shared + 1` decodes as STMT_EXPR instead of STMT_ASSIGN (probed
in isolation), so `shared` is not yet fully usable as an ordinary
identifier in statement position. The bridge scenario's drafted entry
point is `parse_and_build_module_scoped` (the former private
`parse_and_build_module` wrapper is not importable from a spec).

## Verification guidance and troubleshooting

If a tripwire goes RED, the drafted `shared` grammar now parses: restore
the commented drafted assertions in that scenario, replace the rejection
probe, and re-run. If scenario five fails, check whether `shared = ...`
decodes as STMT_ASSIGN before assuming the fixture is at fault -- tag
STMT_EXPR means the statement was swallowed as a bare expression.
"""
# Lifecycle: research: doc/01_research/language/host_gpu_lane/later_gpu_host_grammar.md ; plan: doc/03_plan/language/gpu_fpga/sycl_parity_unified_kernel_plan_2026-06-13.md ; architecture: doc/04_architecture/gpu_dynamic_backend_full_offload.md ; design: doc/05_design/compiler/backend/backend_architecture.md
# @cover src/compiler/10.frontend/core/parser_decls.spl
# @manual_section: GPU shared declaration source reachability

use std.spec.step

use std.spec.{describe, it, expect}
use compiler.frontend.core.ast.*
use compiler.frontend.core.ast_stmt.{flat_stmt_pools_dump, flat_stmt_pools_restore, stmt_reset}
use compiler.frontend.core.ast_traversal.{flat_stmt_child_exprs}
use compiler.frontend.core.flat_pool_codec.{FlatPoolReader}
use compiler.frontend.core.parser.{parser_error_count, parser_has_errors, parser_init_with_path}
use compiler.frontend.core.parser_decls.{parse_module_body}
use compiler.frontend.core.types.{span_end, span_start}
use compiler.frontend.flat_ast_bridge.{parse_and_build_module_scoped}
use compiler.frontend.parser_types.*

fn parse_shared_flat(source: text) -> [i64]:
    parser_init_with_path(source, "shared_gpu_decl.spl")
    parse_module_body()
    val decls = module_get_decls()
    decl_get_body(decls[0])

fn shared_decl_still_rejected() -> bool:
    parser_init_with_path("fn k():\n    shared val probe: i64\n", "shared_gpu_decl.spl")
    parse_module_body()
    parser_has_errors()

describe "GPU shared declaration source reachability", tag: ["only-compiled"]:
    it "maps let and var to mutable SharedVar and val to immutable SharedVal":
